## Avoid repeated full copies of a growing accumulator
Copying the entire result per new item can make construction quadratic. Use a fresh locally owned accumulator or a suitable library operation. Preserve caller-owned/shared data and consider the actual bound before reporting a performance defect.
```python
# bad: each iteration copies all previous entries
result = {}
for row in rows:
    result = {**result, row.key: row.value}
```
```python
# good: this function owns the fresh result
result = {}
for row in rows:
    result[row.key] = row.value
```
This also applies to JS/TS reducer spreads, concat, and slice copies. It is not a ban on reduce, immutable code, filter().map(), or small bounded copies; do not mutate inputs as a workaround.
<!-- src: anti-slop/no-reduce-accumulator-copy, oxc/no-accumulating-spread; adapted 2026-10-09 -->

## Preserve omitted fields when changing object construction
An absent property differs from a present undefined value for enumeration, membership, spreads, and some SDKs. Prove the receiver treats them alike before replacing conditional inclusion with an unconditional field. Conditional empty-object spreads remain valid.
```typescript
// bad when absence is part of the contract
const settings = { deadline: requestedDeadline };
```
```typescript
// good: absence stays absence when no deadline was requested
const settings: { deadline?: number } = {};
if (requestedDeadline !== undefined) settings.deadline = requestedDeadline;
```
Use the existing idiom when it preserves the contract; this rule does not require rewriting valid conditional spreads.
