Validate deployment UID, writable parent ownership, sudo availability and PATH too. When interpreter and fake CLI share a directory, removing that directory from PATH may hide both; invoke a private interpreter alias while isolating the fake CLI.
```python
# bad: root-only success does not prove the service account can publish
subprocess.run(["sudo", "publisher", str(destination)], check=True)
```
```python
# good: exercise the real deployment account and its owned destination
assert os.geteuid() == expected_uid
assert destination.parent.stat().st_uid == expected_uid
subprocess.run([str(publisher), str(destination)], check=True, env=deployment_env)
```

Exercise filesystem fixtures in the process and layout that actually performs the
write. A shallow one-parent fixture, or a restrictive umask left by another test,
does not prove nested directories are private in a remote child. Test the actual
entry point in a fresh subprocess with the deployed umask and realistic depth.
```python
# bad: the shallow fixture inherits another test's private umask
run_remote_fixture("wsl/file")
```
```python
# good: reviewed_fixture invokes the actual remote entry against a temp tree
script = '''
import os
from reviewed_fixture import run_actual_remote_entry
os.umask(0o022)
run_actual_remote_entry("wsl/observations/a/capture/file")
'''
subprocess.run([sys.executable, "-B", "-c", script], cwd=fixture_dir, check=True)
assert all(stat.S_IMODE(p.stat().st_mode) == 0o700
           for p in destination.rglob("*") if p.is_dir())
```
An uncertain or failed publisher attempt stays recorded and is not rerun.
Any metadata-only recovery needs a separately reviewed, journaled procedure.
