## Verify real transport limits in both directions
Exercise representative command and response sizes through the actual transport. A successful exit with truncated output is not a complete capture. Read the transport's limit and completeness metadata; a tiny fake cannot establish either bound.
```python
# bad: a tail-only response can silently lose most of the evidence
capture = transport.run(large_command).stdout
```
```python
# good: stage oversized input separately and require complete output
require_input_within_limit(command, transport.input_limit)
result = transport.run(command, full=True)
require_complete(result)
capture = result.stdout
```

A nested shell can expand a variable or command substitution before the intended
shell sees it, leaving an empty destination and copying into the caller's working
directory. Resolve the private destination in the executing environment and pass
argument vectors and an explicit working directory; verify containment before
the write. Preserve any existing files if a boundary fails.
```python
# bad: an outer shell can consume the inner shell's destination variable
run_nested_shell("copy fixture into $scratch")
```
```python
# good: paths and cwd are concrete in the executing process
scratch = create_private_scratch()
require_outside_workspace(scratch)
shutil.copytree(fixture, scratch / "fixture")
subprocess.run(test_argv, cwd=scratch / "fixture", check=True)
```

## Close active evidence dependencies and semantic claims
Derive active loader dependencies from verified executed source and independently
enumerate the actual approved contract's semantic artifacts and permanent claims,
even when no formal path field mentions them. Traverse references from every
seed to closure, including typed relative paths and large JSON without a
size-based skip. Resolve prior-mirror aliases in their exact path context and
verify their source and digest; unresolved references fail the proposed closure.
```python
# bad: this omits nonformal contract claims and stops at initial references
seeds = discover_active_dependencies(verified_source) | formal_refs(record)
assert seeds <= verified_inventory | verified_prior_mirror_refs
```
```python
# good: contract-specific claims are independent seeds, then traverse all edges
seeds = discover_active_dependencies(verified_source) | formal_refs(record)
seeds |= enumerate_contract_artifacts_and_claims(approved_contract)
closure = walk_all_references(seeds, parse_large_json=True,
                              resolve_typed_relative=True)
assert verify_each_against_inventory_or_contextual_prior_alias(closure)
```
The contract-specific enumerator must name the actual required claims and
artifacts; the traversal must report unresolved edges rather than silently
skip them. A helper name or syntactically closed inventory is not proof that
the real entry path and semantic contract were fully enumerated.
