## Preserve callable bindings through a complete path
A later assignment can shadow a helper in the same scope. Test the complete
path through its later use, not only earlier parsing or a manual diff.
```python
# bad: the list replaces the callable used for the trace
def body(path):
    return read_body(path)
body = decode_page(raw_page)
trace = body(trace_path)
```
```python
# good: the page and helper retain distinct bindings
def body(path):
    return read_body(path)
page_body = decode_page(raw_page)
trace = body(trace_path)
```
Record a manual review as manual; report a passing test only after its actual
completion and exit are verified. Preserve failed attempts as failures.
<!-- src: CI reader local shadow and green-09 test failure, 2026-11-01 -->

## Complete all fail-closed preflight before external effects
When a contract requires every input to pass before tool execution, validate the entire set first. Resolve lexical parent traversal and enforce the permitted root plus symlink/ancestor policy before creating scratch directories; Path.absolute() alone does not establish containment.
```python
# bad: the first tool runs before the second archive is checked
for archive in archives:
    validate_archive(archive)
    run_validator(archive, scratch.absolute())
```
```python
# good: rejected input leaves tools uncalled and scratch absent
destination = validate_contained_absent_path(scratch, allowed_roots)
for archive in archives:
    validate_archive(archive)
create_private_directory(destination)
for archive in archives:
    run_validator(archive, destination)
```
Test a corrupt final input, lexical `..`, symlink ancestors and an existing destination through the real filesystem boundary.

A read-only import can still write bytecode into a protected source tree.
Disable bytecode before loading, verify source bytes before execution, and
check the exact tree afterward. Do not weaken unexpected-file checks to hide
the side effect.
```python
# bad: import may create a cache before the integrity check
module = import_from_protected_tree(path)
check_bundle_strict(bundle)
```
```python
# good: the launcher also uses Python -B
sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
check_bundle_strict(bundle)
source = read_and_verify_digest(path, expected_sha256)
exec(compile(source, str(path), "exec"), private_namespace)
check_bundle_strict(bundle)
```

## Retain authority when a child outcome is unknown
Interrupts, generator finalization and journal failures do not prove a child stopped. Keep canonical ownership until the external outcome is known; logging failure must not release authority. A persistent claim for the same gate prevents another process from repeating an ambiguous write.
```python
# bad: an interrupt or failed journal releases authority over a live child
try:
    wait_for_child(child)
    write_journal()
finally:
    release_owner()
```
```python
# good: the durable claim survives unknown outcomes and logging failures
claim_gate_once(gate_id)
outcome = wait_for_confirmed_child_outcome(child)
write_journal(outcome)
release_owner_after_verified_outcome(outcome)
```
Check the interrupt, close/finalization and journal-error paths, not only ordinary child exits.

A transport or tool-session exit and partial stdout are not the launched
child's OS exit. Retain the actual child completion and private streams, or
record the child outcome as UNKNOWN. A later supplemental observation keeps
its own identity and cannot turn the interrupted attempt into a pass.
```python
# bad: successful output does not prove this child completed successfully
receipt["child_exit"] = tool_session_exit
receipt["passed"] = bool(stdout)
```
```python
# good: only this child's durable completion can establish its exit
outcome = wait_for_actual_child_or_unknown(child)
write_private_receipt(outcome, stdout_path, stderr_path)
if outcome.exit_code is None:
    preserve_unknown_attempt()
```

## Bind metric continuity to authoritative samples and workload identity
Require every expected family and fresh raw sample times after rollout. Positive lookback queries can return old samples; timestamp() around a transformed expression can report evaluation time. Prove timestamp semantics against the real query engine. Keep the corrected authoritative baseline and each observation's actual pod/IP identity; do not repair an empty wrong-job baseline by relabeling it with a replacement's IP.
```python
# bad: a present aggregate does not prove a fresh source sample
assert query("timestamp(sum(service_events_total))")
```
```python
# good: evaluate raw selectors with the captured source identity
for selector in required_raw_selectors(source_identity):
    samples = query(f"timestamp({selector})")
    require_nonempty_sample_times_after(samples, rollout_cutoff)
compare_with_authoritative_baseline(baseline, current)
```
Target continuity follows trusted workload identity and replica multiplicity, not immutable physical pod names or IPs. Preserve URL scheme, port and path semantics and the exact known-down set; establish preexisting replacements from source-bound historical evidence.
```python
# bad: an IP change fails continuity, while sets hide a lost replica
assert {target.url for target in current} == {target.url for target in baseline}
```
```python
# good: trusted ownership maps replacements without erasing counts or URLs
assert workload_target_counts(current, trusted_owners) == baseline_workload_counts
assert known_down_endpoints(current) == baseline_known_down
```

For freshness, bind query evaluation and underlying samples to the same
reader identity and a same-reader time anchor. Wall and monotonic samples used
to bound elapsed capture time must come from the same verified clock domain;
a host wall clock can still step or diverge from its monotonic clock;
an SSH client host's wall clock and a remote monotonic clock are not a pair.
Check both bracket offsets and the elapsed bound without changing thresholds or
adjusting clocks; preserve failed or uncertain attempts and their raw inputs.
```python
# bad: unrelated clock domains can make a valid sample appear future or stale
elapsed = remote_monotonic_after - local_monotonic_before
age = local_wall_now - remote_evaluation_time
```
```python
# good: identity, scalar anchor and paired clock evidence stay source-bound
source = read_source_identity(reader)
before = paired_clock_sample(clock_provider)
samples = read_raw_samples_and_times(reader)
anchor = read_and_validate_time_scalar(reader)
after = paired_clock_sample(clock_provider)
require(read_source_identity(reader) == source)
validate_offsets_and_elapsed(samples, anchor, before, after, limits)
```

Exercise the complete composed rollout gate end to end, including ordinary CI
rollouts and the order of every legacy and replacement guard. A
replacement-aware proof tested alone is not reachable if an earlier stale
literal-identity guard rejects the same input. Preserve the original literal
proof and any failed observation as historical evidence. A separately scoped
replacement proof must bind current Pod and controller UIDs to their trusted
workloads and still enforce health, multiplicity, URL semantics and the exact
known-down set; reject unknown replacements. Do not simply move or remove the
old guard without that proof. Test an authorized replacement and an unknown
replacement or genuine loss through the actual composed entry point.
```python
# bad: the legacy literal guard can fail before the replacement proof runs
legacy_exact_target_check(old_targets, new_targets)
replacement_target_proof(old_targets, new_targets, trusted_owners)
```
```python
# good: keep history and apply a separately proven replacement contract
history = verify_immutable_historical_proof(old_evidence)
current = prove_uid_bound_replacements(new_targets, trusted_owners)
check_target_health_and_multiset(history, current, known_down)
```

## Normalize typed API lists only from trusted type evidence
Real typed-list items may omit apiVersion/kind. Infer them only from the known endpoint or an established core collector; reject an explicit conflicting item type. Fixtures containing complete item type metadata do not test this boundary.
```python
# bad: valid typed-list items are dropped when kind is absent
items = [item for item in response["items"] if item.get("kind") == "Secret"]
```
```python
# good: the verified endpoint supplies missing types; conflicts fail
items = normalize_typed_list(response, expected_api="v1", expected_kind="Secret")
require_no_type_conflicts(items)
```

## Validate numeric values according to the metric type
An empty-window summary quantile can be NaN without invalidating finite companion count/sum metrics. Represent that specific estimate as unavailable after validating its family and companions. Reject infinity, invalid core gauges and error counters; do not permit NaN globally or coerce an unavailable estimate to zero.
```python
# bad: missing estimates become invented measurements
value = 0.0 if math.isnan(value) else value
```
```python
# good: only the documented summary case becomes unavailable
if is_empty_window_summary_quantile(sample, metadata, finite_companions):
    estimate = None
else:
    estimate = require_finite_metric_value(sample)
```

Report every concrete defect inside the assigned review scope, including multiple independent defects in one helper. A narrow scope limits files or responsibilities, not the number of findings. Record review misses as concise bad/good examples in the matching master section, then regenerate repository copies; extend an existing rule when it already covers the mechanism.
```python
# bad: a first finding silently suppresses other verified defects
return verified_findings[:1]
```
```python
# good: return every distinct, verified, in-scope finding
return distinct_in_scope(verified_findings, assigned_scope)
```
